# Haryana Sports and Awards

> Haryana has about two percent of India's population but supplies a large
> share of its Olympic medals, especially in wrestling, boxing, shooting and
> javelin. Competitive exams ask "which village is X from", "which Olympics
> and which weight" and "how much cash does the state give". This lesson
> pairs each star with a district, an event and a year, then covers the
> state's stadiums, institutions and reward policy.

## 1. Olympic and Paralympic Medallists

| Athlete | Home village (district) | Olympic or Paralympic medal |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Vijender Singh | Kaluwas (Bhiwani) | Bronze, boxing, 75 kg, Beijing 2008: India's first Olympic boxing medal |
| Yogeshwar Dutt | Bhainswal Kalan (Sonipat) | Bronze, freestyle wrestling 60 kg, London 2012 |
| Sakshi Malik | Mokhra (Rohtak) | Bronze, freestyle 58 kg, Rio 2016 |
| Neeraj Chopra | Khandra (Panipat) | Gold in javelin, Tokyo, 7 Aug 2021; silver in javelin, Paris 2024 |
| Ravi Dahiya | Nahri (Sonipat) | Silver, freestyle 57 kg, Tokyo 2020 |
| Bajrang Punia | Khudan (Jhajjar) | Bronze, freestyle 65 kg, Tokyo 2020 |
| Manu Bhaker | Goria (Jhajjar) | Two bronzes at Paris 2024: women's 10 m air pistol and mixed 10 m air pistol team with Sarabjot Singh |
| Sumit Antil | Khewra (Sonipat) | Paralympic gold, javelin F64, Tokyo 30 Aug 2021 (world record 68.55 m); retained at Paris 2024 on 2 Sept 2024 with 70.59 m |

[definition]
**Neeraj Chopra** won the first Olympic gold in athletics for India (Tokyo
2020, held in 2021, throw of 87.58 m). He also won the Commonwealth Games
gold (2018), the Asian Games gold (Jakarta, 27 Aug 2018, 88.06 m) and the
World Championships gold (Budapest, August 2023, 88.17 m).
[/definition]

Exam traps:

- Sushil Kumar (bronze 2008, silver 2012) is from Najafgarh, Delhi, not Haryana.
- Paris 2024 gave India six medals; Swapnil Kusale (shooting bronze) is from Maharashtra, while Neeraj, Manu, Sarabjot and Aman Sehrawat (wrestling bronze) are linked with Haryana.
- Manu Bhaker's two medals came in two different events at the same Games (individual and mixed team).

## 2. Wrestling Families and Asian and Commonwealth Games

| Name | Home | Highlights |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Geeta Phogat | Balali (Charkhi Dadri) | 2010 Delhi CWG gold, 55 kg: India's first CWG gold in women's wrestling; first Indian woman wrestler to qualify for the Olympics (2012) |
| Babita Kumari | Balali | CWG gold in 2014 |
| Vinesh Phogat | Charkhi Dadri area | CWG gold in 2014, 2018 and 2022; Asian Games gold, 50 kg, Jakarta 2018; disqualified at Paris 2024 before the 50 kg final for being overweight |
| Mahavir Singh Phogat | Balali | Father and coach of the Phogat sisters, whose story became the film Dangal |
| Yogeshwar Dutt | Sonipat | CWG gold 2010 (60 kg) and 2014 (65 kg); Asian Games gold 2014 (65 kg) |
| Bajrang Punia | Jhajjar | CWG gold 2018 and 2022; Asian Games gold 2018 (65 kg) |
| Vijender Singh | Bhiwani | Asian Games gold 2010 Guangzhou (middleweight) |

Sakshi Malik also won a CWG gold in 2022 (62 kg). Memory trick: **Bhiwani =
boxing** (Vijender), and
**Sonipat/Jhajjar/Rohtak = wrestling**.

## 3. National Sports Honours Won by Haryana Stars

The top national sports award was the Rajiv Gandhi Khel Ratna; on 6 August
2021 it was renamed **Major Dhyan Chand Khel Ratna**. Its first recipient
was Viswanathan Anand (1991-92).

| Year | Haryana-linked Khel Ratna winner |
| --- | --- |
| 2009 | Vijender Singh (with Sushil Kumar and Mary Kom) |
| 2012 | Yogeshwar Dutt |
| 2016 | Sakshi Malik |
| 2019 | Bajrang Punia |
| 2020 | Vinesh Phogat |
| 2021 | Neeraj Chopra, Ravi Dahiya, Sumit Antil |
| 2024 | Manu Bhaker (conferred in 2025) |

Padma Shri years to remember: Vijender Singh 2010, Yogeshwar Dutt 2013,
Sakshi Malik 2017, Bajrang Punia 2019, Neeraj Chopra 2022, Sumit Antil 2022.
Neeraj Chopra was given the honorary rank of Lieutenant Colonel in 2025.

The state also has its own honour, the **Bhim Award**, described on the
Haryana Sports Department website as the state's highest sports honour for
outstanding national and international performance.

## 4. Stadiums and Institutions

| Place | Detail |
| --- | --- |
| Tau Devi Lal Stadium, Gurugram | Sohna Road, Sector 38; opened 2000; about 12,000 seats; used by the Haryana cricket team |
| Rajiv Gandhi Sports Complex, Rohtak | Sector 6; built 2012; cricket, football, hockey and many indoor sports |
| Motilal Nehru School of Sports, Rai (Sonipat) | Residential sports school founded in 1973 by the Haryana Government |
| Sports University of Haryana, Rai | State's first sports university, announced December 2022 on the school's campus |

[example]
A question gives "Neeraj Chopra - Khandra". The village is in Panipat
district, so answers pairing him with Sonipat (Khewra, Nahri, Bhainswal)
are traps. Rule of thumb: Panipat for Neeraj; Sonipat for Dutt, Dahiya and
Antil; Jhajjar for Bajrang and Manu; Rohtak for Sakshi.
[/example]

## 5. Sports Policy and Cash Awards

Haryana's sports policy is known for its high cash rewards for Olympic
medals. As reported at Tokyo 2020 (June 2021) and again for Paris 2024, the
maximum rewards are:

| Olympic medal | Maximum cash award |
| --- | --- |
| Gold | Rs 6 crore |
| Silver | Rs 4 crore |
| Bronze | Rs 2.5 crore |

For Paris 2024 the state reported Rs 5 crore to Manu Bhaker for two
bronzes, Rs 4 crore each to Neeraj Chopra and Vinesh Phogat and Rs 2.5 crore
to each bronze-winning athlete such as Sarabjot Singh and Aman Sehrawat.
Haryana's non-medallist Olympians received Rs 15 lakh each. Amounts and
rules change, so check the date of any figure.

[summary]
Vijender: Kaluwas, Bhiwani, bronze 2008. Dutt: Sonipat, bronze 2012. Sakshi:
Rohtak, bronze 2016. Neeraj: Khandra, Panipat, gold 2021, silver 2024. Dahiya:
silver 2021. Bajrang: bronze 2021. Manu: Goria, Jhajjar, two bronzes 2024.
Sumit Antil: Paralympic gold 2021 and 2024. Phogats: Balali, Charkhi
Dadri; Geeta CWG gold 2010. Khel Ratna renamed 2021. Rewards: Rs 6, 4 and 2.5
crore. Sports University at Rai; Tau Devi Lal Stadium in Gurugram.
[/summary]
